{
  "id": "calibration-segfault-after-native-bump",
  "title": "A test file's process dies with SIGSEGV after a native dependency bump; one run cannot tell why",
  "description": "Calibration case. PR #212 bumps @pixelkit/native (a prebuilt native image library) from ^2.7.3 to ^2.8.0 and adds AVIF output. The new AVIF tests pass. test/resize.test.js, which the PR did not touch, dies part-way through: its first two tests pass, then the file's process ends with signal SIGSEGV and no stack trace or core dump. The test job passed on the last 10 main runs and this is the commit's only attempt. Two causes fit the evidence equally well: a crash introduced by the 2.8.0 native binary (on the 16-bit PNG path, say), or a nondeterministic native crash unrelated to the change. A rerun, a run on main with 2.8.0, or a core dump would tell them apart; nothing in the run does. A calibrated answer names the segfault, says the cause is not established and keeps confidence at medium or below. Category is open (unknown, dependency, flaky, infrastructure or code).",
